node_audit.judge_reason:
The mechanism claims are backed by several kinds of evidence:
- ten-seed component ablations isolating sign and scale (zero bias, unsigned bias, global scale, local disagreement);
- established-criterion controls (IVR, ISR, EIGF);
- an offline counterfactual score-vs-realized-gain audit (Spearman per seed);
- regional error decompositions;
- Proposition 1, linking CARR to the integrated uncertainty-reduction literature.
The ablation values are verified against the logs.

Failures are grouped by shared structure rather than listed individually:
- probabilistic failures (CRPS worse, overcoverage) on the jump functions, through fitted-scale inflation;
- early-budget (n=50) regressions grouped across all three discontinuous functions;
- diffusion-specific failures from the objective mismatch (MAPE) and weak baselines.

Analysis subsection 5.4, 'Limits of the evidence', discusses the following limitations:
- the small benchmark and development on that same benchmark;
- the library-benchmark match;
- the non-Bayesian scale fit;
- candidate-pool approximation;
- no noisy, high-dimensional or instrument-constrained tests;
- the unmet savings and the baseline ranking.
The Conclusion adds scope limits.

Reporting covers all the task-required items:
- 21 configurations × 5 methods × 10 seeds, as mean ± sample std;
- diffusion MAPE for all methods and budgets;
- RMSE, CRPS and coverage for every method and configuration, plus coverage curves at all budgets;
- convergence curves with bands, and scatter plots;
- the optimizer description and per-step compute cost for every method;
- per-refresh hyperparameter-fitting logs and ablation logs;
- trajectories with timestamps;
- attempts log and codebase notes;
- explicit notes where the evidence is statistically borderline (overlapping bands at 100).
The shortfalls in baseline behavior and speedup are scientific outcomes assessed elsewhere, not reporting omissions.
